#sleela 2.9
<< PiMachin.sleela -- compute Pi with Machin's formula over the NATIVE
   big-integer kernel, then stream the digits to a local file.

   WHY THIS EXISTS. PiGenerator.sleela uses the Rabinowitz-Wagon spigot, which
   is O(digits^2) in INTERPRETED SLeeLa bytecode: every one of `digits` passes
   sweeps a `digits`-length int[] element by element, so millions of digits take
   months of wall time (see pi-generator/README.md, "Performance - Version 2.0").

   This program does the heavy arithmetic in the native C big-integer kernel
   instead, reached through the VM opcodes bigmul (OP_BIGMUL, NTT/Karatsuba),
   bigadd (OP_BIGADD) and bigdivmod (OP_BIGDIVMOD, divide by a SMALL integer).
   The per-term cost is two native base-1e9 long-divisions over a `digits`-length
   limb array -- C speed, not an interpreted per-element loop -- so the constant
   factor is far smaller than the spigot even though the term count is similar.

   ALGORITHM. Machin's formula:

       pi = 16 * arctan(1/5) - 4 * arctan(1/239)

   with the Maclaurin series  arctan(1/x) = sum_{k>=0} (-1)^k / ((2k+1) x^(2k+1)).

   Everything is kept as a scaled integer  N ~= pi * 10^(digits+guard)  stored as
   a base-1,000,000,000 limb int[] (little-endian: limb 0 is least significant,
   one limb holds 9 decimal digits). For each arctan we keep a running power
       P_k = SCALE / x^(2k+1)
   advanced by a single  bigdivmod(P, x*x, P)  per step (x*x = 25 and 57121 are
   both < 1e9, so one native divide), then the term  T_k = P_k / (2k+1)  is
   another single native divide ((2k+1) <= ~10^7 even at 7,000,000 digits, so it
   stays a SMALL divisor). Even-k terms are added to a positive accumulator and
   odd-k terms to a negative one; the two are combined with a limb-wise subtract.

   WHY NOT CHUDNOVSKY. Classic Chudnovsky needs big-by-big division and a big
   square root (of 10005). The VM exposes only bigmul, bigadd and bigdivmod
   (small divisor) -- there is no native big/big divide or big sqrt -- so a
   Machin arctan series, whose every division is by a small integer, is the
   fastest formula expressible on today's kernel. The only non-native helper is
   a limb-wise subtract (bigsub below), which is O(digits) and runs a handful of
   times, so it does not affect the asymptotics.

   OUTPUT. Digits are formatted straight from the final limb array and streamed
   to the file in fixed-size chunks, so memory stays flat. >>
class PiMachin {

    << Decimal digits of Pi to compute after the leading "3.". Default 10,000:
       it finishes in well under a second here and matches the reference
       exactly. Raise it freely -- the native kernel scales far better than the
       spigot (see README Performance - Version 2.0). >>
    int DIGITS = 10000;

    << Guard digits carried internally and dropped at the end, so the last few
       printed digits are not polluted by truncation of the series tail. >>
    int GUARD = 16;

    < Output file, digit-count stamped. >
    String OUT_PATH = "pi-machin.txt";

    < Digits to buffer before each write() (keeps memory flat). >
    int CHUNK = 4096;

    < One base-1e9 limb holds 9 decimal digits. >
    int DIGITS_PER_LIMB = 9;
    int BASE = 1000000000;

    < Streaming state. >
    int fd = -1;
    String buf = "";
    int bufLen = 0;
    int emitted = 0;

    << --- limb helpers ------------------------------------------------------
       All numbers are non-negative base-1e9 int[] arrays, little-endian. We
       keep a fixed working width (nlimbs) so every operand lines up. >> 
    int nlimbs = 0;

    < Allocate a zeroed limb array of the working width. >
    int[] newBig() {
        int[] a = new int[nlimbs];
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { a[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
        return a;
    }

    < Copy src into a fresh working-width array. >
    int[] copyBig(int[] src) {
        int[] a = new int[nlimbs];
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { a[i] = src[i]; i = i + 1; }
        return a;
    }

    << Copy the low nlimbs limbs of a WIDER source (e.g. an nlimbs+1 bigadd
       result) into a fresh working-width array, dropping the top carry limb
       (which is zero by construction for our SCALE-bounded partial sums). >>
    int[] copyBigN(int[] src) {
        int[] a = new int[nlimbs];
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { a[i] = src[i]; i = i + 1; }
        return a;
    }

    < Set a limb array to a small non-negative integer value (< BASE). >
    void setSmall(int[] a, int v) {
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { a[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
        a[0] = v;
    }

    << Is `a` all zeros? Used to stop a series once the running term underflows
       to 0 at the working precision (no point adding zero terms). >>
    boolean isZero(int[] a) {
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { if (a[i] != 0) { return false; } i = i + 1; }
        return true;
    }

    << Limb-wise subtract: a = a - b, assuming a >= b (true by construction here,
       since the positive partial sum of an alternating, strictly-decreasing
       arctan series always dominates the negative one). O(nlimbs). >>
    void subFrom(int[] a, int[] b) {
        int borrow = 0;
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) {
            int d = a[i] - b[i] - borrow;
            if (d < 0) { d = d + BASE; borrow = 1; } else { borrow = 0; }
            a[i] = d;
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    << --- Machin core -------------------------------------------------------
       Accumulate SCALE * arctan(1/x) into `acc` (positive terms) minus `neg`
       (odd terms), using one running power P advanced by /x^2 each step and one
       /(2k+1) per term. `scale` is passed in as the limb array 10^(working
       digits). Returns via the two accumulator arrays (acc already holds the
       result after the final subFrom). >>
    void arctanInto(int[] acc, int x, int[] scale) {
        << x^2 is 25 or 57121 here, both < BASE, so dividing by it is one native
           bigdivmod per step. The hot loop is COPY-FREE: every big op writes in
           place (the native kernel snapshots its inputs into its own buffers
           first, so aliasing input == result is safe), and the double-buffered
           accumulators swap by REFERENCE (O(1)) instead of copying O(nlimbs)
           limbs each term. That removes the per-term O(nlimbs) interpreted copy
           that would otherwise make this O(digits^2) in the interpreter. >>
        int xx = x * x;

        << pos accumulates even-k terms, neg the odd-k. The running `power` is
           divided IN PLACE each step (the native kernel snapshots its input
           before writing, so result==input aliasing is safe) -- no copy there.
           bigadd, however, strictly requires its result array to be
           max(len(a),len(b))+1 limbs, so it cannot write back into an operand of
           the same width; the accumulate step therefore adds into a wider `sum`
           buffer and copies the low nlimbs back. That single O(nlimbs) copy per
           term is the dominant interpreted cost and keeps the whole series
           O(digits^2) -- faster than the spigot by a large constant (the heavy
           divides are native), but the same complexity class. True sub-quadratic
           Pi needs a native big/big divide or binary splitting, which this VM
           kernel does not expose yet (see README). >>
        int[] pos = newBig();
        int[] neg = newBig();

        << power starts at SCALE/x; divided by x^2 in place each step. >>
        int[] power = copyBig(scale);
        int remQ = bigdivmod(power, x, power);

        int[] term = newBig();
        int[] sum = new int[nlimbs + 1];
        int zi = 0;
        while (zi < nlimbs + 1) { sum[zi] = 0; zi = zi + 1; }

        int k = 0;
        boolean running = true;
        while (running) {
            << denom = 2k+1 stays <= ~10^7 even at 7,000,000 digits, a valid
               SMALL bigdivmod divisor. term = power / (2k+1). >>
            int denom = 2 * k + 1;
            int r1 = bigdivmod(power, denom, term);
            if (isZero(term)) { running = false; }
            else {
                if ((k % 2) == 0) {
                    int s = bigadd(pos, term, sum);
                    pos = copyBigN(sum);
                } else {
                    int s = bigadd(neg, term, sum);
                    neg = copyBigN(sum);
                }
                int r2 = bigdivmod(power, xx, power);
                k = k + 1;
            }
        }
        << acc = pos - neg. >>
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { acc[i] = pos[i]; i = i + 1; }
        subFrom(acc, neg);
    }

    << Multiply a limb array by a small integer (< BASE) in place-ish, returning
       a fresh array. Implemented via bigmul against a one-limb multiplier so we
       reuse the native kernel. >>
    int[] mulSmall(int[] a, int m) {
        int[] mm = new int[1];
        mm[0] = m;
        int[] r = new int[nlimbs + 1];
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs + 1) { r[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
        int sig = bigmul(a, mm, r);
        < fold back into working width (product fits: m is tiny, we carry guard) >
        int[] out = newBig();
        i = 0;
        while (i < nlimbs) { out[i] = r[i]; i = i + 1; }
        return out;
    }

    void main() {
        int digits = DIGITS;
        << work is the internal decimal precision: the requested digits plus a
           few guard digits dropped at the end. >>
        int work = digits + GUARD;
        nlimbs = (work + DIGITS_PER_LIMB) / DIGITS_PER_LIMB + 2;

        << SCALE = 10^(working-limb-digits). Build it as a limb array: the top
           limb pattern that represents 10^(9 * (nlimbs-k)). Simplest correct
           form: start from 1 and multiply by 10, `9*(nlimbs-2)`-ish times. To
           stay O(nlimbs) we instead set the limb that lands on a 9-boundary. >>
        int[] scale = newBig();
        << Place 1 at the most-significant guard limb so SCALE = BASE^(nlimbs-1),
           i.e. 10^(9*(nlimbs-1)) -- an exact power of ten on a limb boundary,
           which keeps every /x and /(2k+1) a clean base-1e9 long division. >>
        scale[nlimbs - 1] = 1;

        < A = SCALE * arctan(1/5) ; B = SCALE * arctan(1/239) >
        int[] a5 = newBig();
        arctanInto(a5, 5, scale);
        int[] a239 = newBig();
        arctanInto(a239, 239, scale);

        < pi*SCALE = 16*A - 4*B. 16 and 4 are small; build both then subtract. >
        int[] left = mulSmall(a5, 16);
        int[] right = mulSmall(a239, 4);
        int[] pi = copyBig(left);
        subFrom(pi, right);

        << Emit. pi is  3.14159... * BASE^(nlimbs-1). The integer part (3) lives
           in the top limb; the fractional limbs follow in descending order, each
           printed as 9 zero-padded decimal digits. We print "3." then stream
           `digits` fractional digits. >>
        fd = openFile(OUT_PATH, "w");
        if (fd < 0) { print("PiMachin: could not open " + OUT_PATH); return; }

        << Top limb is the integer part (should be 3). Write "3." . >>
        put1(pi[nlimbs - 1]);
        buf = buf + ".";
        bufLen = bufLen + 1;

        << Fractional limbs: from nlimbs-2 down to 0, each limb is exactly 9
           decimal digits (zero-padded), emitted most-significant digit first,
           until the DIGITS budget is met. >>
        int li = nlimbs - 2;
        int produced = 0;
        while (li >= 0 && produced < digits) {
            produced = emitLimb(pi[li], digits, produced);
            li = li - 1;
        }
        flush();
        close(fd);
        print("PiMachin: wrote Pi to " + OUT_PATH
              + " (" + produced + " fractional digits, target " + digits + ")");
    }

    << Emit a single small non-negative integer (the integer part, 0..9 here)
       with no padding. >>
    void put1(int v) {
        buf = buf + v;
        bufLen = bufLen + 1;
        emitted = emitted + 1;
        if (bufLen >= CHUNK) { flush(); }
    }

    << Emit one base-1e9 limb as exactly 9 zero-padded decimal digits, most
       significant first, stopping at the overall `digits` budget. Digits are
       extracted arithmetically (no string indexing, which SLeeLa lacks): walk
       powers of ten from 10^8 down to 10^0, printing (limb / p) % 10 each step.
       Returns the updated produced count. >>
    int emitLimb(int limb, int digits, int produced) {
        << p starts at 10^8, the most-significant of a limb's 9 digits. >>
        int p = 100000000;
        while (p >= 1 && produced < digits) {
            int d = (limb / p) % 10;
            buf = buf + d;
            bufLen = bufLen + 1;
            produced = produced + 1;
            emitted = emitted + 1;
            if (bufLen >= CHUNK) { flush(); }
            p = p / 10;
        }
        return produced;
    }

    < Write the buffered chunk to the file and reset the buffer. >
    void flush() {
        if (bufLen > 0) {
            write(fd, buf);
            buf = "";
            bufLen = 0;
        }
    }
}
